; P11 M2 -- what an Asteroids frame costs once the ship and the shots are in it.
;
;   erall                        <-- REQUIRED, see below
;   load "asteroids   (do NOT run the game)
;   load "p11m2
;   p11m2
;
; M1 measured a rocks-only frame at 60.5 ms and fitted all three points to
; `frame = 30.5 + 2.507 n`.  The design projected M2 at about 72.6 ms -- six
; over -- and priced four levers to claw it back.  The first run of THIS script
; measured 86.4 ms at twelve rocks, nineteen over, with the collision pass alone
; at 20.9 ms against 7.5 estimated: an `item` walk into a twelve-element list
; costs about 115 us, and a three-pass frame did roughly 290 of them, a third of
; the frame, of which about 130 re-read a value another pass had already read.
;
; So the game was re-cut into ONE pass over the rocks that steps, tests and
; draws each one (docs/asteroids-design.md section 15), and this script now
; reads THAT pass apart from the rest of the body, because that is where every
; remaining lever pulls:
;
;   MAX.ROCKS  the rock pass, on its slope
;   segments   the rock pass, on its slope
;   shot cap   the rock pass, through `shot.on` -- timed separately below
;
; The rest of the body -- the ship, the shot ageing, the HUD, `clean` and the
; recycle -- is the frame minus the present minus the rock pass, and it does not
; move with the rock count.
;
; THE WORST CASE, NOT A TYPICAL ONE.  Three shots are live on every frame of
; every point -- the game's ceiling, and the case the budget has to be cut
; against.  Since the fusion an idle shot is parked off the field rather than
; guarded, so a quiet frame costs the same three comparisons a rock; what the
; worst case still buys is that no shot is ever short-circuited by a hit.
;
; HOW THE BOARD IS HELD STILL.  Every rock's `rrad` is set to 0 for the run, so
; no shot ever hits anything: the rock count stays exactly where `setup.level`
; put it for all 300 frames, no rock splits, and no shot is consumed.  Nothing
; in the game is redefined to do this -- it is one list, set once.
;
; The price of that is stated rather than hidden: a zero box rejects every shot
; on `shot.on`'s FIRST comparison, where a real box (26 steps for a large rock)
; passes the x test for about one pair in six and runs a second comparison.  So
; the rock-pass column below is a LOWER BOUND, low by roughly
;
;   36 pairs * 1/6 * one comparison  ~=  0.3 ms
;
; and a frame in which a shot actually connects is CHEAPER than either, because
; the rock it kills is not drawn.  A lower bound is the right side to err on for
; a number that is about to decide which levers get spent.
;
; Manual refresh, not `sync`: the number wanted is the work, not the cadence.
; `sync` would wait out the rest of the frame and report the wait.  That also
; means the figures here are what the frame COSTS, and the game will run at
; `fps` or slower, never faster.
;
; ONE THING THIS OVER-MEASURES, and it is worth knowing which way: the board it
; builds is all LARGE rocks, and that board cannot occur in play.  A level
; spawns `level.rocks` larges and twelve rocks is only reachable by splitting
; down, so a real twelve-rock board is smaller rocks with fewer segments -- 2.9
; ms cheaper at M2's three starting larges, 1.9 ms once levels advance to five.
; The pessimism is deliberate: it is reproducible, it compares with M0 and M1,
; and it errs on the side that does not flatter the budget.
;
; Run it in graphics mode -- `setup.level` leaves the screen there.  In text
; mode `screen_gfx_blit_dirty` returns immediately, so the present column
; reads zero and the frame figure is a Logo body with no present in it, which
; is the correction P9 had to make to its whole first series of numbers.
;
; `erall` is not optional.  MAX_PROCEDURES is a hard 128; the game defines 36
; and this file adds 10, but a workspace still holding another program can run
; out of procedure slots and `load` stops with `out of space` -- the procedure
; table talking, not memory.
;
; The report goes to `p11m2.txt` as well as the screen, because numbers on the
; PicoCalc's display cannot be copied anywhere.  Runs append.

make "p11m2.frames 300

make "p11m2.body (list 0 0 0)      ; ms, mean
make "p11m2.pres (list 0 0 0)      ; ms, mean
make "p11m2.rock (list 0 0 0)      ; ms, mean -- the rock pass alone
make "p11m2.lo (list 0 0 0)        ; ms, whole frame
make "p11m2.hi (list 0 0 0)
make "p11m2.rocks (list 0 0 0)     ; rocks actually on the board, not asked for

; `play.frame` without its `sync`, with a timer around the rock pass.
; THIS MUST STAY IN STEP WITH THE GAME: it is the same statements in the same
; order, and it is spelled out here rather than calling `play.frame` because
; the present has to be timed on its own and `sync` is the last thing
; `play.frame` does.  `test_asteroids.c` pins that this draws what a real frame
; draws and steps what a real frame steps.
;
; The two `ticks` and the two statements around them are in the body figure,
; and cost about 0.15 ms of it.  That is the price of knowing which half is
; over, and it is smaller than the decision it informs.
to frame.body
  local "t
  poll.input
  make "frame.count :frame.count + 1
  step.ship
  step.shots
  clean
  make "t ticks
  step.draw.all
  make "rockpass ticks - :t
  draw.ship
  draw.hud
  reclaim
end

; Three shots, fired 120 degrees apart so they are not one dot travelling
; together, through the game's own `fire` so the turtles are set up exactly as
; play sets them up.
to launch.shots
  repeat :max.shots [
    make "sh (repcount * 120) - 120
    fire
  ]
  make "sh 0
end

; A shot expires after `shot.life` frames.  Topping the life up before each
; frame keeps all three live for the whole run without touching the game's
; countdown: `step.shot` still decrements, it just never reaches zero.  Outside
; the timed body, so it is not in any figure below.
to arm.shots
  repeat :max.shots [.setitem repcount :slife :shot.life]
end

; `init.game` FIRST, then the rock count: init.game resets `level.rocks` to
; `start.rocks`, so setting it before the call is silently thrown away and
; every point measures a three-rock board.  That is exactly what M1's first
; hardware run did -- all three points came back within 0.03 ms of each other,
; and three identical numbers is what gave it away.  `p11m2.rocks` records what
; was actually on the board so the report can never hide it again.
to measure :k :n
  local "b local "p local "c local "one local "t0 local "lo local "hi
  init.game
  make "level.rocks :n
  setup.level
  .setitem :k :p11m2.rocks :rocks.alive
  repeat :max.rocks [.setitem repcount :rrad 0]
  launch.shots
  setrefresh "manual
  make "b 0
  make "p 0
  make "c 0
  make "lo 99999
  make "hi 0
  repeat :p11m2.frames [
    arm.shots
    make "t0 ticks
    frame.body
    make "one ticks - :t0
    make "b :b + :one
    make "c :c + :rockpass
    make "t0 ticks
    refresh
    make "one :one + (ticks - :t0)
    make "p :p + (ticks - :t0)
    if :one < :lo [make "lo :one]
    if :one > :hi [make "hi :one]
  ]
  .setitem :k :p11m2.body :b / :p11m2.frames
  .setitem :k :p11m2.pres :p / :p11m2.frames
  .setitem :k :p11m2.rock :c / :p11m2.frames
  .setitem :k :p11m2.lo :lo
  .setitem :k :p11m2.hi :hi
end

; Printed twice -- once to the file, once to the screen -- so it is one list of
; lines rather than two that can drift apart.  Punctuation is spelled out in
; words: every mark in a printed list is tokenised as a word of its own, so
; `M1+M2` comes out as `M1 + M2` and `--` as `- -`.
; The count printed is the one the board actually carried, read back from the
; game after `setup.level`, not the one this script asked for.
to line.for :k
  pr (se item :k :p11m2.rocks [rocks and 3 shots])
  pr (se [  body] item :k :p11m2.body [ms   present] item :k :p11m2.pres [ms])
  pr (se [  of that, the rock pass] item :k :p11m2.rock [ms at least])
  pr (se [  frame] (item :k :p11m2.body) + (item :k :p11m2.pres)
      [ms   min] item :k :p11m2.lo [ms   max] item :k :p11m2.hi [ms])
end

; What one `recycle` costs.  The game recycles every 25 frames rather than
; every 250, so whatever this is lands inside a 66.7 ms budget every 1.7
; seconds.  Ten of them, after a frame's worth of garbage each, so the figure
; is a recycle with something to do rather than an empty one.
to time.recycle
  local "t0 local "total
  make "total 0
  repeat 10 [
    frame.body
    make "t0 ticks
    recycle
    make "total :total + (ticks - :t0)
  ]
  output :total / 10
end

; Thrust is the only part of a frame that is not in the figures above, because
; it runs only on frames where thrust was the key read and the harness presses
; nothing.  Measured on its own so the worst case -- a player holding thrust at
; full speed, where the clamp branch runs every time -- can be added by hand.
to time.thrust
  local "t0
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 100 [thrust]
  output (ticks - :t0) / 100
end

; What one `shot.on` costs -- the whole shot-cap lever, priced on its own,
; because since the fusion it is three statements inside the rock loop and no
; timer can get between them.  Cap the shots at two and a third of this comes
; off every live rock, every frame.  Measured against the same parked-or-flying
; shots the run itself used, so it is the common path and not a contrived one.
to time.shot.on
  local "t0
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 100 [ignore shot.on 40 40 26]
  output (ticks - :t0) / 100
end

to p11m2.report
  pr [P11 M2 what an Asteroids frame costs with the ship and shots]
  pr (se [frames per point] :p11m2.frames)
  pr []
  line.for 1
  line.for 2
  line.for 3
  pr []
  pr (se [budget at] :fps [fps is] 1000 / :fps [ms])
  pr [M1 rocks only measured 30.5 plus 2.507 n]
  pr [before the rock pass was fused this measured 86.4 ms at 12 rocks]
  pr [fused and still at 15 fps it measured 68.3]
  pr []
  pr (se [one shot.on] :son [ms, times one rock, times every frame])
  pr (se [one thrust] :thr [ms, on frames where it is held])
  pr (se [one recycle] :rec [ms, and the game does one every 25 frames])
  pr []
  pr (se [nodes at start] :n0)
  pr (se [nodes at end  ] :n1)
  pr (se [after recycle ] :n2)
  pr []
end

to p11m2
  local "n0 local "n1 local "n2 local "rec local "thr local "son
  recycle
  make "n0 nodes
  measure 1 6
  measure 2 9
  measure 3 12
  make "rec time.recycle
  make "thr time.thrust
  make "son time.shot.on
  make "n1 nodes
  recycle
  ; Read the recovered figure HERE, not inside the report: printing builds a
  ; `sentence` per line, so a `nodes` evaluated down there reads the report's
  ; own garbage back as if the recycle had under-recovered.  P10's script lost
  ; its first hardware run to exactly that.
  make "n2 nodes
  setrefresh "auto
  textscreen
  open "p11m2.txt
  setwrite "p11m2.txt
  p11m2.report
  setwrite []
  close "p11m2.txt
  p11m2.report
  pr [Also written to p11m2.txt]
end
